minative,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"stochen":{"entries":[["stochen",null,"/ˈʃtɔxən/","Attested since the 16th century. In the 15th already the derivative Stogker (“stoker”). This gk-spelling as well the late attestations suggest an adapted borrowing from Middle Low German stōken, from Proto-West Germanic *stokōn, whence Dutch stoken, English stoke. However, Central Franconian dialects show descendants of this which appear to be native (compare Luxembourgish stachen), so it is also possible that the verb was inherited in northern Central German through Middle High German *stochen and later reinforced by Low German. The simplex did not establish itself in written German, but the frequentative stochern (“to poke”) did.",null,"haben"]],"senses":[[688097,"stochen","verb","to stoke, stir (fire)",0,null,["regional","rare"]]]},"struppigeren":{"entries":[["struppigeren",null,null,null]],"senses":[[162349,"struppigeren","adj","inflection of struppig:",0],[162350,"struppigeren","adj","strong genitive masculine/neuter singular comparative degree",1],[162351,"struppigeren","adj","weak/mixed genitive/dative all-gender singular comparative degree",2],[162352,"struppigeren","adj","strong/weak/mixed accusative masculine singular comparative degree",3],[162353,"struppigeren","adj","strong dative plural comparative degree",4],[162354,"struppigeren","adj","weak/mixed all-case plural comparative degree",5]],"inflections":[["struppigeren","struppig","comparative,genitive,masculine,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"vaterschaften":{"entries":[["vaterschaften",null,null,null]],"senses":[[169723,"vaterschaften","noun","plural of Vaterschaft",0,"f"]],"inflections":[["vaterschaften","vaterschaft","plural"]]},"verruchteste":{"entries":[["verruchteste",null,null,null]],"senses":[[576784,"verruchteste","adj","inflection of verrucht:",0],[576785,"verruchteste","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative feminine singular superlative degree",1],[576786,"verruchteste","adj","strong nomina